  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The most commonly used methodology for twin analysis relies on the simple one-way analysis of variance (Kempthorne and Osborne 1961, Haseman and Elston 1970, Christian et al. 1974, Kang et al. 1974, Eaves et al. 1978, Martin et al. 1978). Separate analyses are performed for monozygotic and dizygotic twins using the layout given in Table 19.1. Since twin groups always consist of two individuals, this is one of the few designs in quantitative-genetic analysis that is always perfectly balanced.",
      "source_page": 595,
      "printed_page": 575,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter19:p002:b5",
        "Genetics_chapter19:p002:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
